amux — AI-native terminal control plane

usage: amux [command] [flags]

commands:
  (bare)     open the workgroup dashboard (native TUI); without a terminal, print this help
  repo       track the repositories agents get worktrees of
  workgroup  create, open and manage workgroups and their agents  (alias: wg, session, ses, workspace, ws)
  agent      commands an agent runs about itself (status, name, raise, wait, done, ...)
  status     print workgroups and their agents, then exit
  top        per-session CPU/memory, top processes and amux's own overhead
  queue      the user question queue: list, answer, dismiss, acknowledge  (alias: q)
  question   answer agents' questions with every feature of their own dialog
  do         drive one daemon control action (the scripting entrypoint)
  refresh    ask the daemon to re-poll its sources now
  doctor     health check: versions, compatibility, dependencies and runtime  (alias: health, check)
  config     show or change amux settings (keybindings, Codex control, updates, ...)  (alias: conf, cfg)
  harness    amux-managed Claude Code / Codex installs  (alias: harnesses)
  sandbox    each agent's private copy of your harness config: drift, promote, reset  (alias: sb)
  auth       central harness logins, delivered to sessions by the daemon
  provide    provide this machine as remote compute (provider mode)
  direct     direct client access (web, Android) over your LAN or VPN (opt-in, host only)
  daemon     run or control the local daemon (restart loads a new binary)
  version    print CLI, daemon and database versions and compatibility  (alias: --version, -v)
  update     install the latest amux release  (alias: upgrade)
  licenses   print bundled dependency licenses and notices  (alias: notices)

flags:
  -h, --help     show help for amux or for the command it follows
  -a, --all      with --help (and no command): print the entire help tree
  --all-help     print the entire help tree: every command, flag, action and example
  -v, --version  print CLI, daemon and database versions (same as amux version)

Set AMUX_SKIP=1 in your shell to bypass auto-launch.

Run 'amux <command> --help' for details. 'amux --help --all' prints everything.
